  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Thunderstorms with severe wind gusts and locally \nheavy rain will be likely into early this evening across \nprimarily the Lower Susq Valley\n\n3 pm EDT update: TSRA have underperformed so far today, likely \nas a result of the airmass unable to completely recover from \nthe early to mid morning MCS and some trailing convection that \nmoved quickly ESE across Central PA and the Lower Susq Valley. \n\nNorthern, lead MCV is exiting our NE CWA attm and our focus will\nturn to the Scent mtns and Lower Susq Valley through 21Z, where\nfocused shear and UVVEL ahead of a second MCV has led to the  \ntallest Refl cores of the day - prompting the issuance of a few\nSVRs and one FFW. \n\nWidespread mid and upper level debris clouds in the wake of this\nline will prevent significant airmass recovery, even though deep\nlayer speed and directional shear (and resultant hodographs) \nare the best there. Some scattered, strong storms are possible\nthere, but latest HREF members point toward mainly a quiet\nevening across NW PA. Best chc for SHRA/TSRA later this evening\nand overnight are across the SW quarter of PA.\n\nWill have to keep a close eye on the eastward extent of the\nnext, MCS tracking SE overnight. A further east track would\nplace parts of our SW zones in the potential of heavy rain and\nFlash Flooding. For now, 6-12 probs for over 1 inch of rain\nacross Cambria and Somerset are quite low, so we'll continue\nwith our high chc to low likely pops and a glancing blow with a\nfew to several tenths of an inch of rain, with the rest of the\nCWA seeing generally less than 0.10 QPF from 11/00 to 11/12Z. \n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Humid with rounds of heavy showers and strong \nthunderstorms focused over southwest Pennsylvania through \nmidweek\n\nModel trends are focusing primary risk for severe storms and\nheavy rain across southwest PA on Tuesday. This area lies\nclosest to the equatorward edge of the westerlies within very\nmoist instability gradient. SPC and WPC have outlooked this \narea (SW PA) with a a level 2/5 severe storm risk and 2/4 \nexcessive rain risk. \n\nRounds of showers and storms remain possible through midweek. \nMultiple shortwave disturbances will traverse the northern tier\nand interact with a slow moving frontal zone. The combination \nof rich low level moisture, instability and repeated forcing \nalong the boundary will support some potential for periodic \nclusters of storms with locally heavy rainfall. WPC excessive \nrain outlooks (EROs) have shifted farther to the south/west and \nonly clip the far southwest portion of the CWA on days 3-5 or \nWed-Fri. \n\nThe end of the week into the start of the weekend looks less\nhumid (more refreshing) as push of relatively cooler and drier\nair accompanies seasonably strong/Canadian high pressure \nbuilding southeast from the Upper Midwest.",
